{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 27, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful ruler who governed the County of Tripoli during the 12th century. He was a key figure in the Crusades and a significant military leader in the Levant. His reign marked a period of both prosperity and conflict for the region.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond III was born around 1100 to Raymond II Count of Tripoli and his wife Countess Adela of Blois. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1130.  He was a skilled military strategist and a capable administrator quickly establishing his authority.  He faced early challenges consolidating power against rival factions and maintaining stability within his territory. His father had established a strong military force and Raymond III continued this tradition.  He proved himself a competent ruler quickly solidifying control over Tripoli.\n\nRaymond III's reign saw significant expansion of the County's territory. He engaged in numerous military campaigns to secure Tripoli's borders and expand its influence. He fought against Muslim forces in Syria and Palestine and also dealt with internal rebellions. He strategically allied with other Crusader states and European powers.  He strengthened Tripoli's defenses building fortifications and improving its infrastructure. He fostered trade and agriculture contributing to economic growth.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Conflicts*  Raymond III's military career was marked by numerous conflicts. He participated actively in the Third Crusade alongside King Louis VII and Emperor Conrad III.  He fought against Saladin's forces and played a crucial role in the siege of Acre.  He also engaged in conflicts with other Crusader states such as the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Principality of Antioch. He often acted as a buffer between the Crusader states and Muslim territories.  His military prowess earned him respect throughout the region and cemented his reputation as a formidable warrior. He skillfully adapted to changing military tactics and technologies.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III's reign was relatively short lasting until his death in 1153. He is remembered as a successful ruler who defended the County of Tripoli against Muslim incursions and expanded its territory. He is also celebrated for his military leadership and his role in the Crusades. After his death his son Raymond IV succeeded him. The County of Tripoli continued to be a significant power in the Levant for several more decades after Raymond III’s passing. He left a lasting impact on the political and military landscape of the region contributing significantly to the Crusader presence in the Middle East. His legacy continues to be studied by historians interested in the Crusades and the history of the Levant.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.1805190145969391}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -4.8874615458771586e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.622600959512056e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.07509470731019974}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.02148974873125553}, {"token": "▁powerful", "logprob": -0.5968805551528931}, {"token": "▁ruler", "logprob": -0.5940437912940979}, {"token": "▁who", "logprob": -0.5694845914840698}, {"token": "▁governed", "logprob": -1.5091681480407715}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.0004051103023812175}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.03069235570728779}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -1.3112935448589269e-05}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "▁during", "logprob": -1.8022834062576294}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.07062172144651413}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.5340746641159058}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0018797124503180385}, {"token": "th", "logprob": -2.384185506798